ZERO WEB NATIVE VIEWER - EXPERIMENTAL PROFILE

The viewer is original Zero code. It reuses and adapts our own Zero Studio
X11 wire protocol and Win32 host-interface implementation. It does not link
Xlib, bundle a GUI toolkit, launch another browser, or execute foreign browser
engine code. Operating-system interfaces remain interoperability boundaries.

Supported native surfaces

Linux x86-64: a local X11 DISPLAY, screen zero, a 24-bit TrueColor root visual
with 32-bit padded BGRX pixels. Xwayland can provide that interface; native
Wayland protocol is not implemented. MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 credentials are read
from the process's XAUTHORITY file or HOME/.Xauthority. A display that already
grants access to this user may accept an empty cookie. Remote DISPLAY names
are rejected. The viewer never opens a TCP X11 connection.

Windows x64: a native Win32 window and direct GDI presentation of our pixel
buffer. Keyboard events use the native queue. Wheel input uses Raw Input;
legacy wheel messages are not counted twice. Native synchronous close is
recognized with IsWindow, including close handled inside PeekMessage.

Both use a fixed 800x600 client canvas, a native pointer cursor, and the same
original raster buffer. X11 publishes fixed minimum and maximum size hints.
The document renderer is responsible for text, colors and all page pixels.

Controls

Up/Down and mouse wheel scroll by 40 pixels. PageUp/PageDown scroll by 520.
Home goes to the start, End to the last full viewport. Scrolling clamps to
0..max(content_height-600,0). Esc and the native close control close the
viewer. It has no address bar, tab manager, link activation or editable page
controls yet. It is a native page-preview frontend, not a complete modern
browser UI.

Integration contract

Include gui-declarations.zero.part with the shared engine declarations, then
append gui.zero.part after successful document processing and initial
rasterization. The block uses @web, @v0..@v31 and 22 persistent @u variables.
Worker frames have their own saved values. The caller owns a valid zeroed
4 MiB arena and initializes the renderer worker before invoking the viewer.

Descriptor fields are little-endian u64 values:
  web+16: error; a nonzero renderer error is preserved.
  web+40,48: viewport width800 and height600.
  web+72: mode1 opens a native window; mode0 skips the entire GUI block.
  web+80: current scroll offset in pixels.
  web+88: content height in pixels.
  web+1024: caller continuation context.
  web+1664: initialized renderer continuation context.
  web+2097152: 800*600*4 bytes of native BGRX raster data.

A successful scroll resumes the existing renderer and presents its pixels.
A renderer failure closes the window without presenting a new failed frame.
Each invocation allocates its own 1 MiB GUI workspace and 256 KiB wire buffer;
these are released when the window closes or startup fails. Native window
and socket resources are closed as well. No web-page byte becomes a host
window procedure, native function pointer, file path or command.

Startup/presentation failures set descriptor error74 unless an earlier error
is already present. Allocation failure exits71. Missing required Win32 DLL,
API symbol or window creation exits20/21/22, respectively. The caller must
supply the valid arena and fixed raster contract; this is not a general
pointer validation or process-isolation boundary.

Checks on 25 November 2026

The original gui-check.zero fixture uses the same GUI block and an original
cooperative test renderer. Its output reports scroll, render count and error.
The own source composer regenerates it with its --gui-check option.
  no argument: interactive navigation fixture.
  no-gui: headless-path fixture; no display is opened.
  fail-render: renderer returns65 on first positive scroll.

Linux native and Windows x64 targets compile with our existing Zero compiler.
10 interactive control scenarios pass on Linux/Xvfb and 10 on Windows/Wine:
Esc, Down, Up-at-start, Down/Up, PageDown, PageDown/PageUp, End/Down-at-limit,
End/Home, wheel-up-at-start/wheel-down, and native window close after scrolling.

Final-target smoke checks on both platforms verify the actual displayed RGB
pixels, wheel scrolling and native close. X11 minimum/maximum canvas hints
are read back. A renderer-error case on each platform closes normally and
preserves error65. Both headless paths pass. Four Linux rejection cases cover
remote DISPLAY, nonzero screen, overlong display number and absent local
server; they fail74 without opening a viewer.

An external Xvfb 1920x1080x24 display, XTest input injection and pixel captures
are validation instruments only. Windows execution was through Wine, not a
native Windows installation. Physical displays, native Windows hardware and
native Wayland have not been tested. These checks concern the frontend; they
do not establish full HTML/CSS/JavaScript, network, security or Chromium
compatibility for the document engine.

Integrated final browser checks

The complete build-final/zero-web and zero-web.exe were run, not only the
frontend fixture. On Linux and Wine, the actual HTML/CSS/JavaScript demo
opened and its entire 800x600 client screenshot matched the original CLI
--render Zpix export pixel-for-pixel after decoding with zero-picture.
The native close control ended each process successfully.

A 40-paragraph original external HTML fixture was opened on each platform.
PageDown changed the visible page; Home restored every pixel of the initial
view; Esc closed the process. All four integrated GUI scenarios exited 0.
The PNG captures are external validation/presentation artifacts, not project
implementation or its internal image format. Native Windows remains untested.
